// TASK 2: Pre-commit Secret Detection
$task_contents[2] = <<<'HTML'
<div class="content-block">
    <h2>Catching Secrets Before They Escape</h2>

    <p>A secret is any credential that grants access: an API key, a database password, a cloud provider token, a signing certificate. Once a secret is committed to git, it exists in the project's history forever, even if the line is later deleted. Removing it from the latest commit does not remove it from history.</p>

    <p>Pre-commit secret detection runs on the developer's own machine, before a commit is even created. Think of it like an airport security checkpoint that happens before boarding, not a search conducted after the plane has already left the gate. Catching the secret here means it never reaches the shared repository at all.</p>

    <div class="warning-box">
        <h4><i class="fas fa-triangle-exclamation"></i> Important Limitation</h4>
        <p>Pre-commit hooks only protect commits made after the hook is installed. Secrets already sitting in existing git history require a separate history scanning tool to find and remove.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>How Secret Detection Works</h3>
