// TASK 5: DAST Against Staging Environments
$task_contents[5] = <<<'HTML'
<div class="content-block">
    <h2>Attacking the Running Application</h2>

    <p>Dynamic Application Security Testing takes the opposite approach to SAST. Instead of reading source code, DAST interacts with a running application from the outside, the same way a real attacker would, without any access to the underlying code. This is often called black box testing.</p>

    <p>DAST runs against a staging environment rather than production, so it can safely attempt real attacks such as cross site scripting, authentication bypass, and configuration checks without risking customer data or uptime. Because it tests the deployed system as a whole, it can catch issues that only appear once everything is running together, something SAST cannot see by reading code alone.</p>

    <div class="info-box">
        <h4><i class="fas fa-info-circle"></i> Why Staging, Not Production</h4>
        <p>DAST tools actively send attack payloads and can trigger errors, unexpected states, or performance issues. Running these tests against a staging environment protects real users while still testing a fully deployed application.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>SAST vs DAST</h3>
    <table class="content-table">
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th>Aspect</th>
                <th>SAST</th>
                <th>DAST</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td>What it examines</td>
                <td>Source code, not running</td>
                <td>A live, running application</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Access required</td>
                <td>Access to the codebase</td>
                <td>Only a running URL, like an attacker</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Runs at</td>
                <td>Pull request stage</td>
                <td>After deployment to staging</td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
</div>
HTML;

// TASK 6: IaC Security Scanning and Designing Balanced Gates
$task_contents[6] = <<<'HTML'
<div class="content-block">
    <h2>Securing the Infrastructure Itself, Then Balancing the Gate</h2>

    <p>Infrastructure as Code scanning examines infrastructure definition files, such as those describing cloud storage, networking, and access permissions, before that infrastructure is ever provisioned. It catches misconfigurations like an open security group, a publicly readable storage bucket, or missing encryption, in the same way inspecting architectural plans catches a structural flaw before construction ever begins.</p>

    <p>Having five scanners is only useful if the pipeline reacts to their findings sensibly. A well designed gate blocks the deployment only for findings serious enough to justify stopping work, and logs everything else for later review. A smoke detector sensitive enough to catch a real fire but not so sensitive that burnt toast sets it off is doing its job correctly.</p>

    <div class="warning-box">
        <h4><i class="fas fa-triangle-exclamation"></i> Important Limitation</h4>
        <p>A gate that blocks on every low severity finding trains developers to see security as an obstacle. Teams under enough pressure will eventually disable the gate entirely rather than work around it.</p>
    </div>

